@* Route table for the Blazor UI.

   TFND-128 landed: Blazor is the only front end. There is no SPA fallback
   behind this router any more, which means an unmatched URL now reaches
   NotFound below rather than being answered with index.html — a typo used to
   look like a working page that failed to load. *@
@* Authorization is enforced per COMPONENT here, not at the endpoint.

   The host applies a FallbackPolicy of RequireAuthenticatedUser to every
   endpoint (AuthExtensions), which is the right default for the API but the
   wrong mechanism for a UI: it also gates _framework/blazor.web.js, so an
   anonymous visitor cannot even boot the circuit that would show them a
   sign-in page. It also answers a browser with a JSON 401 instead of a
   challenge.

   So the Blazor endpoint is AllowAnonymous (see Program.cs) and pages carry
   [Authorize] themselves. Per ADR 0002 the real gate is the Application layer
   — a page that forgets [Authorize] renders a shell and still cannot read
   anything, because authorization is decided beneath it, not here.

   That gap was closed in TFND-126 by FrameworkAssetAuthorization, an
   IAuthorizationMiddlewareResultHandler that lets /_framework and /_content
   through AFTER endpoint selection. It was NOT closed by opening the
   FallbackPolicy globally, which would have removed the default that makes a
   forgotten [Authorize] on an API endpoint harmless. *@
@inject NavigationManager Nav

<Router AppAssembly="@typeof(Routes).Assembly">
    <Found Context="routeData">
        <AuthorizeRouteView RouteData="@routeData" DefaultLayout="@typeof(MainLayout)">
            <NotAuthorized>
                @* Both router fallbacks need their own PageTitle. They are
                   rendered INSTEAD of the page component, so the page's
                   <PageTitle> never runs and HeadOutlet emits nothing at all
                   — an untitled document, which is WCAG 2.4.2 Page Titled.
                   Our own axe-core leg caught this on the anonymous root.

                   It is not a corner case: this is what every signed-out
                   visitor following a deep link sees, and an untitled tab
                   reads as the bare URL to a screen reader and to anyone
                   with more than a few tabs open. *@
                <PageTitle>Not signed in — tamp.findings</PageTitle>
                @* Carries the current URL through, so signing in returns the
                   reader to the deep link they followed rather than dumping
                   them on the portfolio. That is the whole point of having
                   addressable URLs. *@
                <div class="stack">
                    <h1>Not signed in</h1>
                    <Card>
                        <p>You need to sign in to see this.</p>
                        <a class="btn btn--primary" href="@SignInHref">Sign in</a>
                    </Card>
                </div>
            </NotAuthorized>
        </AuthorizeRouteView>
        <FocusOnNavigate RouteData="@routeData" Selector="h1" />
    </Found>
    <NotFound>
        @* Inside the layout, deliberately. A bare 404 page strands the reader
           with no navigation, and the most common cause of one here is a
           mistyped or stale deep link — someone who still has somewhere to
           go. *@
        <LayoutView Layout="@typeof(MainLayout)">
            @* See the note on NotAuthorized above — same reason. NotFoundPage
               carries its own title, but this inline fallback is a separate
               render path and does not inherit it. *@
            <PageTitle>Nothing at this address — tamp.findings</PageTitle>
            <div class="stack">
                <h1>Nothing at this address</h1>
                <Card>
                    <p><code class="mono">@CurrentPath</code> does not match any screen.</p>
                    <p class="field__help">
                        If this was a bookmark, the client or project in the URL may have been renamed
                        — the name is part of the address, so a rename moves the page.
                    </p>
                    <a class="btn btn--primary" href="@PortfolioHref">Back to the portfolio</a>
                </Card>
            </div>
        </LayoutView>
    </NotFound>
</Router>

@code {
    private string CurrentPath => "/" + Nav.ToBaseRelativePath(Nav.Uri);

    // Fully qualified: this component IS called Routes, so the bare name binds
    // to the component rather than to the URL scheme.
    private static string PortfolioHref => Tamp.Findings.Web.Routing.Routes.Portfolio;

    private string SignInHref =>
        $"/signin?returnUrl={Uri.EscapeDataString("/" + Nav.ToBaseRelativePath(Nav.Uri))}";
}
